# Netlify _headers file — controls caching for deployed assets.
#
# Problem this fixes: quiz-engine.js / quiz-style.css / *.html are
# referenced by static filenames, so browsers can keep serving an
# OLD cached copy after a new deploy. Since JS and schema changes
# have been shipped together throughout this project, a stale cached
# quiz-engine.js running against the current live Supabase schema
# causes exactly the kind of partial breakage (name shows, XP/Star
# Points don't) that clearing site data or logging out "fixes" —
# because both force a fresh fetch of everything.
#
# IMPORTANT: HTML pages use `no-store`, NOT `must-revalidate`.
# `must-revalidate` only governs the normal HTTP cache — it does
# NOT reliably stop Chrome's back/forward cache (bfcache) from
# restoring a full in-memory snapshot of the page (JS state and
# all) without ever re-contacting the server. This is exactly what
# was still happening on mobile after the first version of this
# file: backgrounding the browser and returning to it, or using
# back/forward navigation, restored an old in-memory copy of
# quiz.html/quizzes.html instead of reloading — desktop testing
# rarely hits this path since full refreshes are more common there.
# `no-store` is the strongest signal to make Chrome do a real fresh
# load every time, on any device, for any navigation type.
#
# JS/CSS files stay on `must-revalidate` — that part was never the
# problem (bfcache concerns the page document itself, not its
# subresources), and `must-revalidate` still lets the browser skip
# a full re-download when the file genuinely hasn't changed (via a
# 304 Not Modified), which `no-store` would prevent.

/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-store

/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
